1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,650
One location on the Yamuna.

2
00:00:01,690 --> 00:00:04,340
Two completely different kinds of
evidence.

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:07,970
And one question: what does the evidence
actually support?

4
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:09,548
We're not touring features.

5
00:00:09,588 --> 00:00:13,407
This is one investigation of one place —
the Yamuna at ITO Bridge,

6
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:15,745
in Delhi. The dashboard is in live mode,

7
00:00:15,785 --> 00:00:19,662
and everything you're about to see is read
live from a FHIR server,

8
00:00:19,702 --> 00:00:21,475
scoped to a single dataset tag.

9
00:00:21,515 --> 00:00:25,450
That tag is how we know these records are
ours on a server that many

10
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:26,210
people share.

11
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,388
Same place, two kinds of evidence.

12
00:00:28,428 --> 00:00:33,001
Above the line: water the environment
agency measured — faecal coliform,

13
00:00:33,041 --> 00:00:37,037
BOD, oxygen. Below it: case counts the
health system notified —

14
00:00:37,077 --> 00:00:38,638
acute diarrhoeal disease.

15
00:00:38,678 --> 00:00:41,393
Notice they don't arrive on the same
clock.

16
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:45,172
The water is measured daily; the cases are
reported weekly.

17
00:00:45,212 --> 00:00:48,760
That difference matters in a moment, so
keep it in mind.

18
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,522
The dashboard surfaces what deserves
attention:

19
00:00:51,562 --> 00:00:53,755
a reading above a screening reference.

20
00:00:53,795 --> 00:00:55,636
Open it and you get two things —

21
00:00:55,676 --> 00:00:58,339
the actual FHIR Observation that was
measured,

22
00:00:58,379 --> 00:01:01,865
and the rule it was screened against, with
its stated basis.

23
00:01:01,905 --> 00:01:06,038
That "live-evidence" reference is a
dashboard-level link built from the

24
00:01:06,078 --> 00:01:06,802
station data;

25
00:01:06,842 --> 00:01:10,622
the record underneath really is a FHIR
Observation on the server.

26
00:01:10,662 --> 00:01:14,207
And the "2 500" you see is the CPCB
bathing-water criterion —

27
00:01:14,247 --> 00:01:16,910
quoted from the code, not recalled by a
model.

28
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:20,970
Before we go further: everything here is
one shared place.

29
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:23,000
Co-location is not causation.

30
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:23,700
Hold that.

31
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:27,219
Let's ask two questions a surveillance
officer actually asks.

32
00:01:27,259 --> 00:01:29,412
Is this a one-off, or is it sustained?

33
00:01:29,452 --> 00:01:32,296
And does it look the same everywhere on
the river,

34
00:01:32,336 --> 00:01:34,085
or is there a pattern along it?

35
00:01:34,125 --> 00:01:36,450
I'll investigate ITO over the last month.

36
00:01:36,490 --> 00:01:37,547
Watch what it does.

37
00:01:37,587 --> 00:01:40,724
It fetches the screening criteria first —
deliberately,

38
00:01:40,764 --> 00:01:43,208
so it never quotes a threshold from
memory.

39
00:01:43,248 --> 00:01:46,558
It ranks the wards, then it draws the
river in flow order:

40
00:01:46,598 --> 00:01:48,926
Wazirabad upstream, then ITO, then Okhla.

41
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:51,005
See the step between the first two —

42
00:01:51,045 --> 00:01:53,546
that's where the load appears on this
reach.

43
00:01:53,586 --> 00:01:57,358
And the persistence strip answers "one-off
or sustained": for ITO,

44
00:01:57,398 --> 00:01:59,265
it's sustained across the window.

45
00:01:59,305 --> 00:02:03,019
Every figure in that answer is checked
against the data the tools

46
00:02:03,059 --> 00:02:06,080
actually returned, and the receipt is the
green chip.

47
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,427
Here's the part I'd stake the project on.

48
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:10,946
The gap between the two peaks is nine days
—

49
00:02:10,986 --> 00:02:12,835
but look at what it calls itself:

50
00:02:12,875 --> 00:02:16,213
a descriptive offset between two maxima,
not a correlation.

51
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:19,362
In this window there are only four weekly
case reports.

52
00:02:19,402 --> 00:02:22,854
You cannot compute a meaningful
correlation from four points,

53
00:02:22,894 --> 00:02:25,544
and the software says so instead of
pretending.

54
00:02:25,584 --> 00:02:27,090
That is the honesty I mean.

55
00:02:27,130 --> 00:02:29,606
So where did those ITO records come from?

56
00:02:29,646 --> 00:02:32,183
Let's send one reading down the real path.

57
00:02:32,223 --> 00:02:36,417
Watch the stages: the raw payload is
validated into a typed envelope,

58
00:02:36,457 --> 00:02:40,405
screened against the prototype references
before any conversion —

59
00:02:40,445 --> 00:02:44,210
so an exceedance is raised even if the
server is unreachable —

60
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:47,216
then mapped to FHIR, tagged with the
dataset tag,

61
00:02:47,256 --> 00:02:50,039
wrapped in a transaction Bundle, and
uploaded.

62
00:02:50,079 --> 00:02:52,616
Here the same reading raises a HIGH alert,

63
00:02:52,656 --> 00:02:56,605
and you can see the resources it becomes:
a Device, Observations,

64
00:02:56,645 --> 00:02:57,893
Locations, Specimens.

65
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:02,250
That's the identical code path a real
sensor or a broker message takes.

66
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:03,798
Sensors arrive over MQTT.

67
00:03:03,838 --> 00:03:06,645
Volunteer surveys arrive over a message
queue.

68
00:03:06,685 --> 00:03:10,731
Public-health returns arrive over HTTP, as
JSON or as a CSV batch.

69
00:03:10,771 --> 00:03:14,445
Different transports — but once inside,
the same validation,

70
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:18,345
the same screening, the same FHIR mapping,
and the same tagged,

71
00:03:18,385 --> 00:03:19,459
idempotent upload.

72
00:03:19,499 --> 00:03:23,421
That's what makes water evidence and
health evidence land in one

73
00:03:23,461 --> 00:03:24,659
interoperable store.

74
00:03:24,699 --> 00:03:28,745
Back where we started — same location,
same two kinds of evidence,

75
00:03:28,785 --> 00:03:33,016
but now we know where every record came
from and how far the evidence

76
00:03:33,056 --> 00:03:36,607
lets us go. Everything you've seen came
from live records:

77
00:03:36,647 --> 00:03:39,764
we used only what still makes sense with
live data,

78
00:03:39,804 --> 00:03:43,355
and left the prototype's mock-only screens
out of the cut.

79
00:03:43,395 --> 00:03:47,626
OneAquaHealth makes heterogeneous
environmental and population-health

80
00:03:47,666 --> 00:03:50,535
evidence interoperable, visible and
traceable —

81
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:53,754
while staying honest about what it cannot
establish.

82
00:03:53,794 --> 00:03:56,664
Synthetic data, prototype screening
references,

83
00:03:56,704 --> 00:04:00,873
and a shared demo server — the method is
real, the readings are not.

84
00:04:00,913 --> 00:04:05,331
Co-located records, a descriptive pattern,
a signal worth investigating.

85
00:04:05,371 --> 00:04:08,673
Not causation. Confirmatory sampling is
the next step,

86
00:04:08,713 --> 00:04:11,211
and that decision stays with the officer.

87
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:13,254
One river. Two kinds of evidence.

88
00:04:13,294 --> 00:04:17,402
A signal worth investigating — and the
discipline to say only that.

89
00:04:17,442 --> 00:04:18,640
Thanks for watching.
